          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.152,
            0.58,
            0.171
          ],
          "content": "We have seen that $(x + 3)(x + 4) = x^{2} + 7x + 12.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.175,
            0.449,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Also}\\;(x+6)(x+7)=x^{2}+13x+42.",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.199,
            0.725,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": "Generalise the pattern to get an expression for $\\left(x+a\\right)\\left(x+b\\right)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.237,
            0.885,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "Now consider the case where we have a rectangle of sidelengths $2 x + 3$ and $3 x + 1$ , as shown in Fig. 4.8. What can you say about its area $\\left(2x+3\\right)\\left(3x+1\\right)?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.313,
            0.711,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.247,
                0.618,
                0.75,
                0.636
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.8: Using algebra tiles to represent $( 2 x + 3 ) \\times ( 3 x + 1 )$"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.654,
            0.883,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "Fill in the blanks with the appropriate expressions to make the equation true."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.264,
            0.699,
            0.739,
            0.719
          ],
          "content": "( p x + a ) ~ ( \\overline { { { q } } } x + b ) = ( \\underline { { { \\hphantom { x } } } } \\hphantom { x } \\hphantom { x } ) \\chi ^ { 2 } + ( \\underline { { { \\hphantom { x } } } } \\hphantom { x } \\hphantom { x } \\hphantom { x } ) \\chi + \\underline { { { \\hphantom { x } } } } \\hphantom { x } \\hphantom { x } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.727,
            0.762,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "Also, verify your answer using the distributive property."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.779,
            0.795,
            0.802
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.6 Factorisation Without Using Algebra Tiles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.812,
            0.886,
            0.91
          ],
          "content": "Consider the algebraic expression $x ^ { 2 } + 7 x + 1 2$ which was obtained by multiplying the linear terms $x \\: + \\: 3$ and $x + 4$ . We also saw in Fig. 4.7 that 7x was split as $3 x   +   4 x$ so that a rectangle could be formed using the algebra tiles. But how do we achieve this ‘splitting of the $x \\; \\mathrm { t e r m } ^ { \\ast }$ without using tiles?"
        }
      ]
    }
